}}The '''Center for Autism and Related Disorders, Inc.''' ('''CARD''') is a business that administers [[applied behavior analysis]] (ABA) to [[Autism|autistic]] people.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2023-06-14 |title=Pocketing Money Meant for Kids: Private Equity in Autism Services |url=https://cepr.net/publications/pocketing-money-meant-for-kids-private-equity-in-autism-services/ |access-date=2025-05-25 |website=CEPR |language=en-US}}</ref>

==History==
CARD was founded in 1990 by [[Doreen Granpeesheh]], a former graduate student of [[Ole Ivar Lovaas|Ole Ivar Løvaas]], the UCLA psychology professor who popularized the use of ABA on autistic children.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=2013-01-09 |title=Families cling to hope of autism 'recovery' |url=https://www.latimes.com/local/autism/la-me-autism-day-three-html-htmlstory.html |access-date=2025-05-17 |website=Los Angeles Times |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Bowman |first=Rachel A. |last2=Baker |first2=Jeffrey P. |last3=on behalf of Duke University School of Medicine |date=2014-03-01 |title=Screams, Slaps, and Love: The Strange Birth of Applied Behavior Analysis |url=https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article-abstract/133/3/364/32244/Screams-Slaps-and-Love-The-Strange-Birth-of?redirectedFrom=fulltext?autologincheck=redirected |journal=Pediatrics |volume=133 |issue=3 |pages=364–366 |doi=10.1542/peds.2013-2583 |issn=0031-4005|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Gibson |first=Margaret F. |last2=Douglas |first2=Patty |date=2018-10-16 |title=Disturbing Behaviours: Ole Ivar Lovaas and the Queer History of Autism Science |url=https://catalystjournal.org/index.php/catalyst/article/view/29579/23427 |journal=Catalyst: Feminism, Theory, Technoscience |language=en |volume=4 |issue=2 |pages=1–28 |doi=10.28968/cftt.v4i2.29579 |issn=2380-3312|doi-access=free }}</ref>

=== Acquisition by Blackstone (2018–2019) ===
In May 2018, the [[Blackstone Inc.|Blackstone Group]] acquired CARD in a leveraged buyout valued at approximately US$600–700 million, one of the largest transactions in the behavioral-health sector.<ref>{{cite web |title=Blackstone to Buy Center for Autism and Related Disorders |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/blackstone-to-buy-center-for-autism-and-related-disorders-1523648367 |website=Wall Street Journal |access-date=19 November 2019 |date=13 April 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Yuk |first1=Pan Kwan |title=Blackstone acquires autism care specialist CARD |url=https://www.ft.com/content/5c9bc19a-3f46-11e8-b9f9-de94fa33a81e |website=Financial Times |access-date=19 November 2019 |date=13 April 2018}}</ref>
<ref name="ProtoLife">{{Cite web |date=2024-02-01 |title=Autism and Private Equity: A Cautionary Tale |url=https://proto.life/2024/02/autism-and-private-equity-a-cautionary-tale/ |access-date=2025-08-11 |website=proto.life |language=en-us}}</ref> Granpeesheh stepped down as CEO in 2019 but remained on the company’s board. Blackstone planned to scale CARD to over 500 clinics using data analytics and centralized management.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bryant |first=Bailey |date=2020-10-22 |title=Bolstered by Recent Tech Investments, CARD Gears Up for Growth in 2021 |url=https://bhbusiness.com/2020/10/22/bolstered-by-recent-tech-investments-card-gears-up-for-growth-in-2021/ |access-date=2022-05-18 |website=Behavioral Health Business |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Center for Autism and Related Disorders Announces Senior Leadership Changes |url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/center-autism-related-disorders-announces-191900949.html |access-date=2022-05-18 |website=finance.yahoo.com |date=2 February 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="CEPR">{{cite report |title=Pocketing Money Meant for Kids: Private Equity in Autism Services |publisher=Center for Economic and Policy Research |author=Rosemary Batt; Eileen Appelbaum; Quynh Trang Nguyen |date=21 June 2023 |url=https://cepr.net |pages=43–49}}</ref>

Former staff later reported that after the acquisition, corporate leadership reduced centralized clinical training and replaced experienced clinicians with managers from outside behavioral health, leading to concerns about quality of care and high employee turnover.<ref name="ProtoLife" /><ref name="CEPR" />

=== Private equity era: growth and decline (2019–2023) ===

==== Growth and labor issues ====
By 2020, CARD operated around 230–250 centers nationwide. However, employees reported high turnover (over 150% annually) and low wages relative to workload.<ref name="CEPR" />  
====Unionization efforts====
In May 2019, while it was still under the ownership of Blackstone, employees at CARD's [[East Portland, Oregon|Portland East]] location voted to [[Unionization|unionize]], the first ABA clinic in the [[United States]] to do so. Prior to the vote, Granpeesheh appeared in person to plead with workers to vote against unionizing, and CARD hired [[Union busting|anti-union]] consultants to intimidate staff. CARD permanently closed the Portland East location in January 2021, citing the impact of the [[COVID-19 pandemic]]. The company subsequently dismissed employee speculation that the Portland East location was targeted for closure because it had unionized.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-02-01 |title=Autism and Private Equity: A Cautionary Tale |url=https://proto.life/2024/02/autism-and-private-equity-a-cautionary-tale/ |access-date=2025-08-11 |website=proto.life |language=en-us}}</ref>

==== Ethical and clinical concerns ====
The [[COVID-19 pandemic]] disrupted in-person services, and CARD’s telehealth expansion proved unsustainable. Critics and former staff alleged that under private equity ownership, clinical practices were increasingly standardized and profit-driven, reducing individualized care.
CARD’s practices under private equity ownership were criticized for:
*Inflating treatment hours to maximize billing.
*Reducing supervision ratios (1 BCBA per 25–30 clients).
*Prioritizing preschool-age clients with higher insurance reimbursement.
These trends reflected a broader pattern of “financialization of care” identified by a CEPR report<ref name="ProtoLife" /><ref name="CEPR" />

The 2023 Center for Economic and Policy Research (CEPR) report concluded that private equity buyouts in autism services, exemplified by CARD, resulted in “reduced staffing, increased debt loads, and lower quality of care.”<ref name="CEPR" />

=== Clinic closures and bankruptcy (2022–2023) ===
Beginning in mid-2022, CARD abruptly closed operations in ten states—including Oregon, Michigan, Missouri, and Rhode Island—citing “unsustainable reimbursement rates.”<ref>{{cite news |title=Centers for Autism and Related Disorders Closing Clinics in Oregon |work=KEZI 9 News |date=14 July 2022 |url=https://www.kezi.com/news/centers-for-autism-and-related-disorders-closing-clinics-in-oregon/article_1b170dbe-03c9-11ed-82d1-4ff398e06735.html}}</ref>

In June 2023, CARD filed for [[Chapter 11 bankruptcy]] protection, reporting US$82 million in losses and US$245 million in debt.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/blackstone-owned-autism-treatment-provider-files-for-bankruptcy-fcaf010a|title=Blackstone-Owned Autism Treatment Provider Files for Bankruptcy|date=June 12, 2023|access-date=June 12, 2023|website=Wall Street Journal|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Autism treatment center gets US bankruptcy court approval for $48.5 million sale |url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/autism-treatment-center-gets-us-bankruptcy-court-approval-485-mln-sale-2023-07-26/ |access-date=30 August 2023 |publisher=Reuters |date=26 July 2023}}</ref><ref name="CEPR" />  
Under a court-approved stalking horse agreement, Granpeesheh’s company '''Pantogran LLC''' repurchased CARD’s remaining 130 centers for about US$25 million—less than 10% of its 2018 valuation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Autism Treatment Center Files for Bankruptcy, Plans Sale to Founder |work=Yahoo! News |date=12 June 2023 |url=https://news.yahoo.com/autism-treatment-center-files-bankruptcy-214146796.html}}</ref>

The CEPR report concluded that Blackstone’s debt-loading and overexpansion turned CARD from a sustainable clinic network into “a cautionary example of financialized health care.”<ref name="CEPR" />

== Legacy ==

CARD is widely recognized for establishing ABA as the predominant evidence-based treatment for autism. Its research initiatives and software platforms, such as Skills, influenced global clinical training and insurance models.<ref name="CEPR" /><ref>{{cite news |last=Zarembo |first=Alan |title=Selling success: Autism therapy gains ground |newspaper=Los Angeles Times |date=December 13, 2009 |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2009-dec-13-la-me-autism13-2009dec13-story.html |quote=Granpeesheh, a former graduate student of Ivar Lovaas, founded the Center for Autism and Related Disorders (CARD), which has become one of the largest ABA-based treatment providers and a key player in expanding insurance coverage for applied behavior analysis.}}</ref><ref>{{cite report |last=Batt |first=Rosemary |author2=Eileen Appelbaum |title=Pocketing Money Meant for Kids: Private Equity in Autism Services |publisher=Center for Economic and Policy Research |date=June 21, 2023 |url=https://cepr.net/report/private-equity-in-autism-services/ |quote=Blackstone’s buyout of the Center for Autism and Related Disorders (CARD) in 2018 illustrates how CARD—one of the largest ABA providers in the U.S.—played a pivotal role in expanding applied behavior analysis as the dominant evidence-based autism therapy and in developing major software tools like Skills™ to support individualized treatment and insurance compliance.}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Ponsford |first=Matthew |author2=Ruairí Casey |title=What the bankruptcy of CARD teaches us about private equity in autism care |magazine=Proto.life |date=July 2023 |url=https://proto.life/2023/07/private-equity-autism/ |quote=Founded by psychologist Doreen Granpeesheh, CARD helped establish applied behavior analysis (ABA) as the gold-standard intervention for autism through its research, training, and software systems such as Skills™, which influenced clinical and insurer protocols worldwide.}}</ref>

While CARD’s model is credited with expanding access to autism treatment, it has been criticized by neurodiversity advocates for promoting intensive behavioral modification and neglecting autistic self-advocacy perspectives.<ref name="ProtoLife" />  
The organization’s financial collapse under private equity ownership has become a case study in debates about ethics and oversight in the corporatization of health care.<ref name="CEPR" />

Following the 2023 bankruptcy proceedings of CARD, founder Doreen Granpeesheh reacquired the organization and resumed leadership of a smaller, downsized CARD network.<ref>{{cite news |last=Knauth |first=Dietrich |title=Autism Treatment Center Files for Bankruptcy, Plans Sale To Founder |work=Reuters via Yahoo! News |date=June 12, 2023 |url=https://news.yahoo.com/autism-treatment-center-files-bankruptcy-214146796.html |quote=The Center for Autism and Related Disorders filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in June 2023. The filing followed an agreement under which founder Doreen Granpeesheh’s Pantogran LLC would assume $245 million in debt and repurchase CARD’s assets for $25 million.}}</ref><ref>{{cite report |last=Batt |first=Rosemary |author2=Eileen Appelbaum |title=Pocketing Money Meant for Kids: Private Equity in Autism Services |publisher=Center for Economic and Policy Research |date=June 21, 2023 |url=https://cepr.net/report/private-equity-in-autism-services/ |quote=By June 2023, Blackstone’s leveraged buyout had culminated in CARD’s bankruptcy. Founder Doreen Granpeesheh subsequently reacquired the company, assuming its liabilities and reorganizing it as a smaller network of clinics.}}</ref><ref name="ProtoLife" />

==Documentary==
CARD co-produced (with Granpeesheh) and distributed ''[[Recovered: Journeys Through the Autism Spectrum and Back]],'' a 2008 [[Documentary film|documentary]] about four children that the organization claims completely [[Cure#Related terms|"recovered"]] from autism and co-occurring intellectual disability as a result of the intensive, longterm ABA they administered,<ref name=":0">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nro0LsqiP90 |title=Recovered: Journeys Through the Autism Spectrum and Back |date=2021-12-05 |last=Autism Live |access-date=2025-05-17 |via=YouTube}}</ref> despite the fact that there is no known cure for autism.<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Myers SM, Johnson CP |date=November 2007 |title=Management of children with autism spectrum disorders |journal=[[Pediatrics (journal)|Pediatrics]] |volume=120 |issue=5 |pages=1162–1182 |doi=10.1542/peds.2007-2362 |pmid=17967921 |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Levy SE, Mandell DS, Schultz RT |date=November 2009 |title=Autism |journal=[[The Lancet]] |volume=374 |issue=9701 |pages=1627–1638 |doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(09)61376-3 |pmc=2863325 |pmid=19819542}}{{Erratum|https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(11)61666-8/fulltext|doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(11)61666-8|checked=yes}}</ref> In the film, Granpeesheh stated, "There's a lot of scientific research that shows children recover from autism, and yet, still, the medical community in general is not aware of how prevalent recovery is. More than half of the children receiving intervention at an early age recover completely."<ref name=":0" /> The film was directed and edited by Michele Jaquis in collaboration with Granpeesheh and won the Best Documentary award at the 2008 Director's Chair Film Festival.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.thedirectorschairtv.com/Fest2008/Award2008.html |title=The Director's Chair Film Festival 2008 |access-date=2009-04-11 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090107133450/http://www.thedirectorschairtv.com/Fest2008/Award2008.html |archive-date=2009-01-07 |url-status=dead }}</ref> 

